 "case:accusation.successTitle": "The final move recorded",
 "case:briefing.0": "The Bitola Open has filled the House of Culture on Širok Sokak with sixty-four adult players, folding boards, paper score sheets and the dry ticking of chess clocks. It is the biggest open tournament in the country, and tonight’s final round will decide the title.",
 "case:briefing.1": "At 18:24, Boris Vasilev, sixty-two, the tournament’s chief arbiter and a former national champion, was found dead in the arbiter’s room behind the stage. He had been struck with the brass round bell used to start and end each round. Nothing valuable appears to be missing.",
 "case:briefing.2": "Round Eight ended at 17:48. The players were given a scheduled break before the final pairings at 19:00. Some went to the foyer café, some stayed in the analysis room, and some walked out onto Širok Sokak. The hall was full of witnesses, but nobody agrees on who was where.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Boris had spent the afternoon arguing with players, officials and the tournament sponsor. He kept a sealed envelope on his desk and told the assistant arbiter that it would be opened before the prize ceremony. Nobody will say what was inside.",
 "case:briefing.4": "The police have postponed the final round and sealed the arbiter’s room. The trophy is still under a cloth, the clocks are still set for the next game, and every adult who mattered is still in Bitola. Find out who killed Boris Vasilev, how they crossed a public room without being noticed, and what Boris was about to put on the record.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "By midnight, the House of Culture was empty except for police, federation officials and the unfinished final pairings. The brass bell sat in an evidence bag on Boris’s desk, beside the cold coffee and the blank result slip.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "The federation suspended the Bitola Open’s results while it reviewed the Round Eight records. Maja Velkova refused the provisional title until every score was checked, and the players agreed to return the following month for a clean final round.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "Sara Dinevska received her arbiter’s certificate the next winter. She kept Boris’s green desk lamp in the club office and used it whenever someone asked her to correct a result without a written reason.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "The tournament now begins with two bells: one for the players, and one for the record. The second bell is never rung unless every clock, score sheet and signature agrees.",
