 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.0": "Who held the route",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.1": "Who had something to lose",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.question": "Who connects the service route to Boris’s report?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.title": "The player who left the room",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dragan_accounts.text": "Boris was checking the club accounts before signing the tournament report.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dragan_key.text": "Viktor was allowed to sign out SERVICE 2 because he knew the building and had an old federation badge.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_elena_boris.text": "Elena believed Boris was going to report the grant figures, not the old chess appeal.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_elena_grant.text": "Elena needed the final round and prize ceremony to satisfy the city grant.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_maja_result.text": "Maja and Katerina agreed a draw, signed the sheet and left the board.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_maja_viktor.text": "Viktor was gone from the analysis room when Maja returned from the foyer.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_nikola_break.text": "Nikola photographed the public hall throughout the break and never went behind the stage.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_nikola_report.text": "The 2018 result was changed after the signed sheet, and a Viktor-coached player benefited.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sara_found.text": "Sara found Boris at 18:24 after he failed to answer her bell-test call.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sara_result.text": "Sara did not write the replacement Board Four result slip.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_viktor_alibi.text": "I stayed in the analysis room until after six, helping Maja prepare for the final round.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_viktor_result.text": "The Board Four result was corrected because someone copied the wrong result, not because I changed it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_accounts.lines.0": "Why did Boris want the club accounts before signing the tournament report?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_accounts.lines.1": "Because he wanted every receipt copied twice. The prize fund was intact and the volunteers were paid.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_accounts.lines.2": "There was a delay with one supplier. That is an accounting problem, not a murder motive.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_accounts.lines.3": "I was downstairs when he died. Elena saw me at the registration table.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_accounts.prompt": "Why was Boris checking the club accounts?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_keys.lines.0": "Dragan, why did Viktor have a service key?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_keys.lines.1": "Because he used to run the federation events here. He knows the building, and I trusted him to get a cigarette from the courtyard.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_keys.lines.2": "The rule says only officials should sign out a service key. The old badge system is not a fortress.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_keys.lines.3": "I did not see him return it. By the time I checked the sheet, the line was blank.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dragan_keys.prompt": "Who could sign out SERVICE 2?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_boris.lines.0": "What did Boris say he would report before the prize ceremony?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_boris.lines.1": "He said the tournament records were untidy. Boris called every untidy thing a threat to chess.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_boris.lines.2": "I assumed he meant the grant figures. I did not know about Viktor’s old appeal.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_boris.lines.3": "I wanted the event to continue. That is not the same as wanting Boris dead.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_boris.prompt": "What did Boris want to report?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_grant.lines.0": "Elena, why did the grant folder say the final round had to go ahead?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_grant.lines.1": "Because the city gave us money for a full event, not a half-finished afternoon with police tape on the stage.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_grant.lines.2": "Boris threatened to delay the ceremony over seven missing signatures. He was right to ask, but he enjoyed making the question public.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_grant.lines.3": "I was at my table in the foyer. Players came to me for badges, coffee and complaints. You can ask any of them.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_elena_grant.prompt": "Why were the attendance figures important?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_board.lines.0": "Maja, what happened in your Round Eight game?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_board.lines.1": "Katerina offered a draw after eleven moves. I accepted it. We both signed the sheet and left the board.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_board.lines.2": "I did not ask anyone to change the result. A half point is not worth turning a tournament into a fraud.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_board.lines.3": "Viktor said Boris would make a scene, but I thought he meant an argument at the board.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_board.prompt": "Tell me about Board Four.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_viktor.lines.0": "Was Viktor in the analysis room at 18:06, as he says?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_viktor.lines.1": "He was there when I arrived. He put the Board Four position on the demonstration board and told me to stop looking at the results.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_viktor.lines.2": "Then I went to the foyer for water. When I came back, he was gone and the service door was shut.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_viktor.lines.3": "I assumed he had gone to find Boris. I did not assume he meant to hurt him.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_maja_viktor.prompt": "Was Viktor with you?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_2018.lines.0": "Nikola, what were you researching about the 2018 championship?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_2018.lines.1": "A result changed after the players signed their sheet. Boris objected, then withdrew the objection before the prize ceremony.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_2018.lines.2": "The player who benefited was coached by Viktor. That does not prove Viktor ordered it, but it explains why Boris kept the file.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_2018.lines.3": "Boris promised me a copy of his new appeal after the final round. I was waiting for the document, not stealing it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_2018.prompt": "What happened in 2018?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_break.lines.0": "Where were you between 17:55 and 18:17?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_break.lines.1": "In the hall, photographing the empty boards and the result board. I wanted the atmosphere before the final round began.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_break.lines.2": "My camera has the time in its files. I was never behind the stage.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_break.lines.3": "I saw Viktor’s jacket missing from the analysis room, but I thought he had gone outside for air.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_nikola_break.prompt": "Where were you during the break?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_correction.lines.0": "The signed sheet says a draw. Who entered the later 1–0 result?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_correction.lines.1": "I did not. Boris had written that no result could be altered without a written appeal.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_correction.lines.2": "The replacement slip was already on the board when I returned from the foyer. I assumed Boris had approved it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_correction.lines.3": "I should have checked the signatures. I was trying to keep sixty-four people from asking me the same question.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_correction.prompt": "Who changed the Board Four result?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_found.lines.0": "Sara, tell me exactly what happened at 18:24.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_found.lines.1": "I was returning to the stage to test the bell. Boris had not answered my call through the public door.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_found.lines.2": "The room was quiet. His lamp was on, his phone was off the cradle and the service door was closed.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_found.lines.3": "I touched nothing except the telephone. I called the police and waited in the foyer.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sara_found.prompt": "What happened when you found Boris?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_break.lines.0": "Viktor, where were you between the end of Round Eight and the discovery of Boris’s body?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_break.lines.1": "In the analysis room. Maja had a difficult final position and I was helping her see the exchange.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_break.lines.2": "The players came and went, but I stayed by the main board. Ask Maja. She was there until the foyer filled.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_break.lines.3": "I walked out for coffee after six. I came back before anyone knew there was a problem.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_break.prompt": "Where were you during the break?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_result.lines.0": "Why did Board Four change from a draw to a win for Maja?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_result.lines.1": "It did not change. Someone copied the wrong result, saw the error and corrected it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_result.lines.2": "Maja was the better player. The board showed that even if the paperwork did not.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_result.lines.3": "Boris loved a rule when it helped him control a room. He would have corrected the slip himself if he had been given time.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viktor_result.prompt": "What happened to the Board Four result?",
 "evidence:evidence.analysis_notebook.detail": "A black notebook left on the analysis table.\n\nViktor has copied the Board Four position and written: ‘18:06 — the rook cannot take because the king is pinned.’ The sentence is written over a faint erased line. The actual Board Four sheet has no rook on that square; the rook was moved on the demonstration board after the game ended.\n\nThe page is the only place in the analysis room that records 18:06. Viktor says he wrote it while discussing the game with Maja.",
 "evidence:evidence.analysis_notebook.name": "Viktor’s analysis notebook",
 "evidence:evidence.analysis_notebook.summary": "Viktor says the 18:06 note was written in the analysis room, but the page describes a position he could not have seen there.",
 "evidence:evidence.appeal_envelope.detail": "The envelope was found inside the lining of Boris’s desk chair after the police searched the room. It is addressed to the National Chess Federation and marked ‘Open before prize ceremony’.\n\nBoris writes that Board Four’s signed draw was changed to 1–0 after the players left the table. He says the alteration was not an innocent correction and that Viktor Markovski had instructed the players to accept the result before the game began. Boris intends to attach the older 2018 report and request Viktor’s suspension as a coach.\n\nThe last line reads: ‘The title must be decided over the board, not by the person who controls the paperwork.’",
 "evidence:evidence.appeal_envelope.name": "The federation appeal",
 "evidence:evidence.appeal_envelope.summary": "Boris’s sealed appeal says he intended to report an arranged result and name the person who altered the tournament record.",
 "evidence:evidence.arbiter_statement.detail": "Statement by Sara Dinevska, assistant arbiter.\n\n‘I took the Round Eight sheets to the hall tray at 17:48. Boris told me he was going through Board 4 before the final pairings. At about 17:50 he went into the arbiter’s room. I stayed at the results board until the players began asking for coffee.\n\n‘At 18:22 I called through the public door because the bell-test had not been done. Boris did not answer. I opened it at 18:24 and found him beside the desk. The service door was closed. I did not touch the bell.’",
 "evidence:evidence.arbiter_statement.name": "Sara’s first statement",
 "evidence:evidence.arbiter_statement.summary": "Sara says she saw Boris alive at 17:50 and found him when he failed to answer her bell-test call at 18:24.",
 "evidence:evidence.back_corridor.detail": "Photographs of the service corridor behind the stage.\n\nThe passage is narrow and painted the same dark green as the stage wall. SERVICE 1 opens toward the analysis room; SERVICE 2 opens into the arbiter’s room. The paint on SERVICE 2’s latch has a fresh scrape at shoulder height. A short black wool fibre is caught in the latch grease.\n\nThere is no camera in the corridor. The public hall would hide the sound of a door closing whenever the café machine or the crowd was active.",
 "evidence:evidence.back_corridor.name": "The service corridor",
 "evidence:evidence.back_corridor.summary": "The corridor gives a direct route from the analysis room to Boris’s door without crossing the public hall.",
 "evidence:evidence.bell_log.detail": "Sara’s round-control notes.\n\n17:45 · final moves called\n17:48 · bell rung; clocks stopped\n17:50 · Board Four sheets delivered to Boris\n17:52 · bell checked on stage; base dry and clean\n18:15 · bell missing when Sara returned for the test\n18:24 · Boris found\n\nThe 18:15 entry is written in Sara’s blue ink. No one signed for removing the bell.",
 "evidence:evidence.bell_log.name": "The round-bell log",
 "evidence:evidence.bell_log.summary": "The bell rang at 17:48 and was still on the stage table when Sara left it at 17:52.",
 "evidence:evidence.board_four_sheet.detail": "A photograph and inventory of the demonstration board.\n\nThe position is consistent with the signed Board Four sheet except for the black king and a white rook. They have been moved to squares that make the final position look like a forced win for White. A dry-erase mark beside the board reads ‘1–0?’ in Viktor Markovski’s handwriting.",
 "evidence:evidence.board_four_sheet.name": "The Board Four position",
 "evidence:evidence.board_four_sheet.summary": "The demonstration board shows the quick draw’s final position, but two pieces have been moved since the players signed the sheet.",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_receipt.detail": "Receipt from the foyer café.\n\nSaturday 17 April · 18:08:32\n1 × double espresso\nCard ending 2284 · V. Markovski\n\nThe server remembers Viktor asking for the receipt folded in half. The espresso was collected from the counter, not delivered to the analysis room. The counter is on the public side of the stage, less than a minute from SERVICE 1.",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_receipt.name": "The café receipt",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_receipt.summary": "Viktor paid for a double espresso at 18:08, after claiming he never left the analysis room.",
 "evidence:evidence.clock_control.detail": "The analogue clock Boris used to time the break.\n\nThe left hand was set to fifteen minutes when the end-of-round bell rang at 17:48. The clock was found on the floor beside the desk, stopped at 18:06. Its glass has a new crescent-shaped impact mark, and a trace of brass is caught in the crack.\n\nThe clock cannot identify the killer by itself. It records the moment something struck or knocked it, inside the estimated death window.",
 "evidence:evidence.clock_control.name": "The stopped break clock",
 "evidence:evidence.clock_control.summary": "Boris’s small tournament clock was wound at 17:48 and stopped at 18:06 with a fresh impact mark on its glass.",
 "evidence:evidence.coat_check.detail": "The numbered coat-rack sheet and a photograph taken at 18:16.\n\nViktor’s jacket was checked on hook 12 at 15:21. At 18:16 the hook was empty, although his tournament badge still hung from it. The jacket reappears on the hook after the police arrive. Its right cuff has a fresh pale mark shaped like the edge of a brass bell base.\n\nThe fibre from SERVICE 2 is black wool with a thin brown thread woven through it. Viktor’s jacket has the same brown thread at the cuff.",
 "evidence:evidence.coat_check.name": "The missing jacket",
 "evidence:evidence.coat_check.summary": "Viktor’s jacket was absent during the break, and its black wool matches the fibre on the service latch.",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_items.detail": "Photographs of the arbiter’s desk.\n\nA green lamp was on. The results ledger is open to Board Four, with the provisional 1–0 slip clipped beneath the signed ½–½ sheet. Boris’s coffee is cold but not finished. The telephone handset is off its cradle. A shallow drawer contains paper dust and a clean rectangle where a thick envelope had been.",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_items.name": "Boris’s desk",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_items.summary": "Boris was reviewing the Board Four result when he was attacked; his sealed envelope is missing.",
 "evidence:evidence.entry_ledger.detail": "Registration and access ledger for Saturday.\n\n17:48 · Round Eight ends\n17:52 · Viktor Markovski signs out SERVICE 2\n17:56 · Maja Velkova leaves the analysis room for the foyer\n18:02 · Elena Stojanovska at sponsor table\n18:04 · Nikola Koteski photographs the empty boards\n18:15 · Sara Dinevska returns to stage\n18:18 · SERVICE 2 returned without initials\n\nThe ledger is written in Sara’s hand, except for the Viktor entry, which is signed in thick black ink.",
 "evidence:evidence.entry_ledger.name": "The attendance ledger",
 "evidence:evidence.entry_ledger.summary": "The public record confirms who was inside during the break and who had a staff badge.",
 "evidence:evidence.hall_plan.detail": "The building plan, printed for the fire marshal.\n\nThe public hall opens onto the foyer and the stage. Behind the stage is a service corridor with two numbered doors: SERVICE 1 opens toward the analysis room; SERVICE 2 opens into the arbiter’s room. The corridor is not visible from the boards or the café counter. Both service doors close automatically, but only SERVICE 2 is normally locked.",
 "evidence:evidence.hall_plan.name": "The House of Culture plan",
 "evidence:evidence.hall_plan.summary": "The arbiter’s room can be reached from the analysis room through a staff corridor behind the stage.",
 "evidence:evidence.journalist_notes.detail": "Pages from Nikola Koteski’s chess column notebook.\n\n‘2018: V. Markovski’s player wins after Board Four result corrected from draw to win. Boris objected, then withdrew the objection. Ask why.’\n\nA later note reads: ‘Boris has sealed copy. He will speak after prize ceremony, not before. Do not print until he confirms.’\n\nNikola’s camera metadata places him in the public hall at 17:55, 18:04 and 18:17.",
 "evidence:evidence.journalist_notes.name": "Nikola’s notebook",
 "evidence:evidence.journalist_notes.summary": "Nikola was researching the 2018 appeal and expected Boris to give him a document after the final round.",
 "evidence:evidence.old_match_report.detail": "Federation report, National Championship 2018.\n\nBoard Four’s signed score sheet recorded a draw. An amended result slip awarded a win to a player coached by Viktor Markovski. The arbiter’s objection was withdrawn before the prize ceremony, and no disciplinary hearing followed.\n\nBoris Vasilev’s signature appears at the bottom beside the words: ‘Insufficient evidence for a formal complaint.’ A handwritten note in a different ink says: ‘Not insufficient. Unwelcome.’",
 "evidence:evidence.old_match_report.name": "The 2018 match report",
 "evidence:evidence.old_match_report.summary": "The older report describes the same kind of result alteration that Boris intended to report again.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_report.detail": "Preliminary report by Inspector Marija Ilievska, filed at 19:05.\n\nVictim: Boris Vasilev, 62, chief arbiter of the Bitola Open.\nLocation: arbiter’s room behind the stage.\nEstimated death: between 17:55 and 18:10.\nCause: a single blunt-force injury to the left side of the head.\n\nThe brass round bell is missing from the stage table. The public door to the room was unlocked, while the service door was closed but not latched. No window was open. Boris’s wallet, phone and watch remain on him. The final round has been postponed.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_report.name": "The police report",
 "evidence:evidence.police_report.summary": "Boris Vasilev was killed in the arbiter’s room during the break before the final round.",
 "evidence:evidence.result_slips.detail": "Two result slips for Board Four.\n\nOriginal slip: Maja Velkova — Katerina Ilievska · ½–½ · signed by both players at 17:38.\nReplacement slip: Maja Velkova — Katerina Ilievska · 1–0 · no player signatures · entered at 18:17.\n\nThe replacement slip uses blue-black ink and the same squared-off ‘V’ found in Viktor Markovski’s notebook. Sara says she did not write it and Boris would never accept an unsigned correction.",
 "evidence:evidence.result_slips.name": "The result slips",
 "evidence:evidence.result_slips.summary": "The provisional 1–0 slip was written after the signed draw and placed on the results board during the break.",
 "evidence:evidence.round8_scores.detail": "Official score sheets from Round Eight, signed by both players.\n\nBoard 4 · Maja Velkova — Katerina Ilievska\nMove 11: ½–½\nBoth signatures are written at 17:38. The game lasted nine minutes and ended before the other top boards.\n\nIn the margin, Boris has written: ‘Both players confirm draw. Do not alter without written appeal.’",
 "evidence:evidence.round8_scores.name": "The Round Eight score sheets",
 "evidence:evidence.round8_scores.summary": "The signed sheet for Board 4 records a draw, although the results tray contains a later slip awarding Maja a full point.",
 "evidence:evidence.scoreboard_photo.detail": "Nikola Koteski’s photograph of the results board, timestamped 17:51.\n\n1. Maja Velkova · 6½\n2. Katerina Ilievska · 6\n3. Petar Naumov · 6\n4. Aleksandar Ristevski · 5½\n\nBoard 4 is marked with a red question mark. The paper beside it says: ‘Awaiting Boris’s check.’ Maja is the only player who can win the title with a draw in the final round.",
 "evidence:evidence.scoreboard_photo.name": "The standings photograph",
 "evidence:evidence.scoreboard_photo.summary": "A photograph taken at 17:51 shows Maja Velkova half a point ahead, with one result still marked provisional.",
 "evidence:evidence.service_key_log.detail": "The Bitola Chess Club’s key sheet, clipped under the registration desk.\n\nSERVICE 1 · 15:05 · Sara Dinevska · stage check · returned 15:16\nSERVICE 2 · 17:52 · Viktor Markovski · courtyard access · returned 18:18\n\nThe return line has no initials. The key was found on the foyer radiator, still warm from someone’s pocket. The club’s rule says only arbiters and the club president may sign out service keys, but Viktor used to manage the building for the federation and had been given a spare badge.",
 "evidence:evidence.service_key_log.name": "The service-key log",
 "evidence:evidence.service_key_log.summary": "Viktor signed out SERVICE 2 at 17:52 and it was returned anonymously at 18:18.",
 "evidence:evidence.sponsor_grant.detail": "Application for the Bitola cultural-events grant.\n\nElena Stojanovska’s application promises 64 registered players, a full final round and a prize ceremony covered by local press. The registration book has 64 names, but the attendance sheet has only 57 signatures. Elena has written in the margin: ‘Boris must not cancel the ceremony before the mayor arrives.’\n\nThe folder gives Elena a reason to fear Boris’s report, but it places her at the sponsor table in the foyer during the break.",
 "evidence:evidence.sponsor_grant.name": "The sponsor’s grant folder",
 "evidence:evidence.sponsor_grant.summary": "Elena promised the city a full final round and risked losing funding if the tournament stopped.",
 "evidence:evidence.tournament_schedule.detail": "Bitola Open programme, Saturday 17 April 2027.\n\n15:30–17:45 · Round Eight\n17:48 · end-of-round bell and collection of score sheets\n18:00–18:45 · scheduled break and final-round pairings\n19:00 · Round Nine\n21:30 · prize ceremony\n\nThe printed schedule says that all clocks and result slips must be returned to the arbiters before the break ends.",
 "evidence:evidence.tournament_schedule.name": "The tournament schedule",
 "evidence:evidence.tournament_schedule.summary": "Round Eight ended at 17:48, with the final round scheduled for 19:00.",
 "suspects:suspects.dragan.bio": "Dragan runs the Bitola Chess Club from a small office above a sports shop. He arranged the venue and the volunteer list, and he has promised the federation that the club can host a larger event next year. Boris had asked to see the club accounts before signing the final report.",
 "suspects:suspects.dragan.dismissal": "The club has survived worse than an argument over receipts.",
 "suspects:suspects.dragan.name": "Dragan Petrov",
 "suspects:suspects.dragan.rebuttal": "Boris wanted every number explained, which is his right. I was downstairs with the registration books. If I had wanted him gone, I would not have called him the only honest official in the room.",
 "suspects:suspects.dragan.role": "The chess-club president",
 "suspects:suspects.elena.bio": "Elena owns a stationery business in Bitola and paid for the hall, the boards and the prize fund. She wants the Bitola Open to become an annual event with the city’s backing. Boris threatened to delay the prize ceremony after discovering that the attendance figures in her grant application did not match the registration books.",
 "suspects:suspects.elena.dismissal": "I sponsored a chess tournament. I did not arrange a tragedy.",
 "suspects:suspects.elena.name": "Elena Stojanovska",
 "suspects:suspects.elena.rebuttal": "Boris was going to embarrass me in front of the mayor, and I was angry. But I was in the foyer where half the players could see me. You are looking for a private quarrel in a public room.",
 "suspects:suspects.elena.role": "The tournament sponsor",
 "suspects:suspects.maja.bio": "Maja is the reigning national champion and the only player in the field with a realistic chance of earning an international norm this weekend. She is controlled at the board and exhausted away from it. Boris questioned one of her Round Eight results in front of the other players, while Viktor insists she should ignore the noise and play.",
 "suspects:suspects.maja.dismissal": "A disputed game is not a reason to murder an arbiter.",
 "suspects:suspects.maja.name": "Maja Velkova",
 "suspects:suspects.maja.rebuttal": "Boris was difficult, but he was doing the job everyone else was too frightened to do. I wanted the final round, not his silence.",
 "suspects:suspects.maja.role": "The defending champion",
 "suspects:suspects.nikola.bio": "Nikola writes the chess column for a Bitola weekly and has been collecting stories about the federation since he was a teenager. He was preparing a piece about Boris’s 2018 appeal when the tournament stopped. He says he spent the break photographing the empty boards and waiting for the final pairings.",
 "suspects:suspects.nikola.dismissal": "A journalist’s notebook is not a murder weapon.",
 "suspects:suspects.nikola.name": "Nikola Koteski",
 "suspects:suspects.nikola.rebuttal": "Boris promised me a document, then changed his mind. I was disappointed, not desperate. My camera was in the hall; ask anyone who saw the flash.",
 "suspects:suspects.nikola.role": "The chess journalist",
 "suspects:suspects.sara.bio": "Sara has worked under Boris for three tournaments and is waiting for the federation to certify her as a full arbiter. She knows the result slips, the clock-control sheets and every key in the building. She found Boris at 18:24 when he failed to answer the bell-test call.",
 "suspects:suspects.sara.dismissal": "I was the person trying to keep the tournament legal.",
 "suspects:suspects.sara.name": "Sara Dinevska",
 "suspects:suspects.sara.rebuttal": "I made mistakes in the paperwork because Boris made everyone work too fast. I did not touch him. I found him, called the police and stayed until they arrived.",
 "suspects:suspects.sara.role": "The assistant arbiter",
 "suspects:suspects.viktor.bio": "Viktor won the national title twice and has spent the last decade coaching adult players who cannot afford to waste a season. He is charismatic, proud and still angry that Boris Vasilev became the federation’s trusted arbiter instead of returning to the board. Viktor says he spent the break in the analysis room, helping Maja prepare for the final round.",
 "suspects:suspects.viktor.dismissal": "You are confusing a loud argument with a murder.",
 "suspects:suspects.viktor.name": "Viktor Markovski",
 "suspects:suspects.viktor.rebuttal": "Boris and I disagreed about chess, about the federation and about the way he treated people. I walked out for coffee and came back. That is not a killing.",
 "suspects:suspects.viktor.role": "The former champion and coach"
}
